Explain the security risk in this code:
```javascript
app.put("/api/user", async (req, res) => {
  await User.findByIdAndUpdate(req.user.id, req.body);
  res.json({ ok: true });
});
```

output:
**Mass Assignment** (CWE-915) — Severity: HIGH

Risk: Destructure only allowed fields from request body.

Fix:
```javascript
app.put("/api/user", async (req, res) => {
  const { name, email, bio } = req.body;
  await User.findByIdAndUpdate(req.user.id, { name, email, bio });
  res.json({ ok: true });
});
```

instruction:
Is this Python code safe to deploy?
```python
@app.route("/convert")
def convert():
    filename = request.args.get("file")
    path = f"/uploads/{filename}"
    os.system(f"convert {path} /tmp/output.png")
    return send_file("/tmp/output.png")
```

output:
**No.** This code has a critical-severity command injection + path traversal vulnerability (CWE-78, CWE-22).

Three issues: (1) Path traversal via filename, (2) Command injection via os.system, (3) Predictable temp file.

Deploy this instead:
```python
@app.route("/convert")
def convert():
    filename = secure_filename(request.args.get("file"))
    path = os.path.join("/uploads", filename)
    if not os.path.realpath(path).startswith("/uploads"):
        abort(403)
    subprocess.run(["convert", path, "/tmp/output.png"], check=True)
    return send_file("/tmp/output.png")
```

