Is this C code secure? Explain any issues found:
```c
void parse(char *s) {
    char local[128];
    memcpy(local, s, strlen(s));
}
```

output:
**No — Buffer Overflow** (CWE-119, CRITICAL)

Check length before memcpy to prevent overflow.

The vulnerable pattern is:
```c
void parse(char *s) {
    char local[128];
    memcpy(local, s, strlen(s));
}
```

The safe alternative is:
```c
void parse(const char *s) {
    size_t len = strlen(s);
    if (len >= 128) return;
    char local[128];
    memcpy(local, s, len);
    local[len] = 0;
}
